==================================================================================================
6. BOUNDARIES
==================================================================================================

REPRESENTATION  076 leaves the structural form to 077: no element names, carrier shapes,
                applicability form, field-identification form or cardinality notation are
                prescribed (076 §7, SC-076-G, RF-076-1, RF-076-2). The choices, each a 077
                CHOICE and not a Blueprint or RMS fact:
                (1) applicability as a list of targets, each an explicit model and at most one
                    Kind — the declaration form Artifacts 069 and 073 use for a declared-Kind
                    scope (REPOSITORY CONVENTION). A fixed single Model/Kind pair would decide
                    SC-076-N by syntax; a list with UNRESOLVED shapes decides nothing (§4). The
                    explicit model is an encoding, not the only constitutional one (A-02);
                (2) the declaration form for the model and Kind, not an R → R reference to a
                    MODEL-DEFINITION or KIND-DEFINITION Record, and not both: a second relation
                    would be an invented one (076 RF-076-1, RF-076-5, RF-076-6). The
                    declaration form needs no definition Record to exist first, and no
                    Registry Record identifier while no Registry Kind code is bound (row 117);
                (3) occurrence entries with three parts — field by name, required, and
                    cardinality written L..U — so that the schema side of row 078's split,
                    "cardinality/required", has a checkable representation (076 §7), with no
                    minimum count of entries (O-01; SC-077-O);
                (4) definition as the governed account of the schema, holding RMS §10.2's
                    constraints, relationships and validation references as content, because no
                    source fixes their form or targets (076 SC-076-J): a dedicated element with
                    a fixed target would invent the relation.
                None of these choices is offered as the only representation a governing source
                could later establish. A later governed change of this contract may add one;
                this contract does not pre-empt it.

APPLICABILITY   Any Model or Kind a SCHEMA-DEFINITION names is declared and named by
                declaration reference (076 RF-076-1). Three questions stay apart, and only the
                first is decided here:
                  representability — can the Record carry the scope? Yes, for every shape (§3)
                  declared status — is each named target declared? NATIVE for a model (the
                    closed set); EXTERNAL for a Kind (the owning model's Kind architecture)
                  admissibility — is the shape an authorized applicability? Admissible for one
                    Kind-scoped target (076 §17 A); UNRESOLVED for a Model-level target or
                    several targets (§4 A-08, A-09; SC-077-C)
                No candidate's syntax proves that a Kind is declared. Structural validity is not
                legality: a Record that passes every NATIVE check is PENDING_EXTERNAL until its
                EXTERNAL checks are established, and is never CONFORMS before (§0).

COMPOSITION     A schema composes fields; a field definition owns meaning and domain (row 078
                Done). An occurrence names a field and states its requiredness and cardinality in
                the schema; it carries no meaning, domain or reference. Composing a field
                respects its governed meaning and domain (Artifact 072 §10; 076 RF-076-2) — an
                EXTERNAL obligation, never waived by a NATIVE pass.
                No reference carrier. No source mandates a field-definition reference for every
                occurrence, and this contract defines none (076 RF-076-2). A field occurrence is
                therefore never an illegal reference merely because no FIELD-DEFINITION Record
                resolves for it: it is not a reference. Whether the composed field is governed
                as 072 requires is the EXTERNAL check of O-08.
                Concrete references. RMS §10.3 permits R → R references to Registry
                definitions, including FIELD-DEFINITION Records; they are permitted, not
                required (076 RF-076-2). This contract carries none. Where a later governing
                source establishes one, its target must actually be a FIELD-DEFINITION Record
                and the relation must satisfy Artifact 063 (RB-1, RB-5, RB-6).
                Not 078's closure. Which FIELD-DEFINITION governs a named field within a
                schema's scope, whether field order is significant, and the final overlap
                closure are row 078's, exactly as its row assigns them (076 §8, SC-076-D). This
                contract does not preempt 078 and adds nothing to its Done; if 078's closure
                calls for a different encoding, this contract changes through its own governed
                change, and row 078 is not edited here.

RESOLUTION      A structurally valid Record carries the baseline Record identity that later
                Registry-definition resolution requires (§2): partition, kind and object_id.
                Artifact 077 does not by itself establish the complete id+version resolution
                representation: rows 108–110 retain authority over Registry evolution and version
                representation, and row 115 owns consumer resolution — "consumer resolves by
                id+version". Resolution never relies on slug, which is decoration only.
                applicability, occurrence and definition do not thereby become this Record's
                identity. This contract implements no resolver, lookup, index, cache, storage
                path, fallback or version selection, and defines no lookup of the schema that
                applies to a given Kind; row 114 owns Registry operations —
                "create/resolve/version a definition". Resolution transfers nothing, and
                resolvable is not legal (Artifact 063 §11). (076 RS-076-1 to RS-076-6)

VERSIONABILITY  Nothing here prevents the Registry evolution and versioning model of rows 108–110
                from applying to a SCHEMA-DEFINITION. Artifact 077 defines no version
                identifier, numbering scheme, pinning representation, supersession, replacement
                or deprecation representation, effective date, migration, retention or
                compatibility rule, or temporal lifecycle mechanism; those decisions stay with
                rows 108 (evolution), 109 (versioning, pinning) and 110 (supersession,
                deprecation). Nor does it say whether two Records may define the same schema —
                as versions of one definition, for example — or how such Records relate; that is
                theirs too. This contract is open to whatever Registry-owned representation those
                rows establish, provided it leaves the seven-field envelope unchanged and keeps
                within the Record Model boundaries (P-05, P-06); no World History Record is used
                or required (I-102). Changing or superseding a schema definition does not by
                itself migrate, rewrite or revalidate any Record of any model, and this contract
                neither requires nor forbids a later governed response (076 VS-076-3).
                Versionable therefore means: Artifact 077 does not structurally obstruct the
                evolution and versioning architecture rows 108–110 own, under Registry-owned
                rules and no lifecycle shared with other models (076 SD-10). Absence here is not
                a decision that a SCHEMA-DEFINITION carries no version. (076 VS-076-1 to
                VS-076-3; SC-077-N)

REFERENCES      The one concrete reference obligation this family carries is the applicability
                target (076 RF-076-1): a declaration reference to a declared Record Model and,
                for a Kind-scoped target, to a declared Kind of that model. Neither is an R → R
                reference to a definition Record, an owned edge or a Relationship Record (076
                RF-076-5; I-102). A field occurrence is no reference (§6 COMPOSITION). References
                that definition content carries are bounded by 076 RF-076-3, RF-076-4 and
                RF-076-6 (§3 definition). No W/E/P/V/I domain instance, no domain state and no
                runtime instance is a target, a dependency or an authority (076 RF-076-7 to
                RF-076-9; Artifact 063 RB-2 to RB-4). A reference transfers no ownership,
                admission or authority, is not by itself a semantic dependency, and is not legal
                because it resolves. An undetermined target is not legal: "Undetermined is not
                permitted." (Artifact 063 RB-6). Every reference a Record carries is legal, not
                merely resolvable (076 SD-13). (076 RF-076-1 to RF-076-11)

LAYER           Artifact 062 §6 does not place SCHEMA-DEFINITION: "Their layer placement is not
                established by current sources". No layer is assigned here, no element takes
                its meaning from a layer, and no layer-based dependency rule is applied (076
                SC-076-L; SC-077-L).

EXECUTION       "Registry does not execute schemas." (RMS §10.2). No element of this contract
                parses, loads or applies a schema; runs, records or blocks a check; creates,
                mutates, repairs or migrates a Record; or holds validator state (076 §9 items
                1–9; P-01A). A shared structural validator may read a SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record
                and check another model's Record against it; that is implementation validation
                (RMS §20), and its output is a "DERIVED verdict" (Blueprint §26.3b), never
                Registry content.
                The NATIVE checks of this file are contract statements, not an implemented
                validator. Blueprint §12.15 names JSON Schema as the adopted structural-validation
                realization; this file is not JSON Schema, generates no validator, and does not
                decide what a later realization looks like (SC-077-J).

CONTENT         Constraints, relationships and validation references stay three things, and the
                tiers stay apart: CONSTRAINT-DEFINITION (what must hold), VALIDATION-RULE (how a
                condition is checked) and implementation validation (the running check) are not
                collapsed into this Record or into each other (076 SD-6; RMS §10.6, §20). A
                schema's relationships create no Relationship Record and no runtime relationship
                ownership (076 SD-7; RMS §15). The binder of constraints to validation rules is
                row 113's.

SOVEREIGNTY     Registry governs the definitions; each Record Model owns its Records (Blueprint
                §13.6e; I-105). A SCHEMA-DEFINITION applicable to World Records gives the
                Registry no World Record, and the same holds for E, P, V and I. Conforming is not
                belonging, and failing to conform gives the Registry no authority over a Record
                (076 §13). Whether a model-owned schema artifact — row 184's CHARACTER schema,
                for example — is described by, declared through or independent of a
                SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record is not established; no element binds the two
                (076 SC-076-I; SC-077-G). A declared schema (RMS §10.3) is not thereby a
                SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record, and how a schema acquires declared status is not
                established (076 RF-076-5; Artifact 063 §8.4).

LS              Row 077 carries LS: LS-1 and Why: LS-1. Roadmap PART III defines LS-1 as "every
                Kind spec ↔ its Registry KIND-DEFINITION. ATOMIC-PAIR." A SCHEMA-DEFINITION
                structural contract is neither a Kind specification nor a KIND-DEFINITION, and
                the Registry's own fourteen pairs are 064 ↔ 075 (Roadmap §0.7). This file is not
                a lockstep partner: 076 ↔ 077 is no pair, and no LS-1 meaning is attached to the
                family (076 §15; SC-077-B).

MATERIALIZATION This file is an authored source. A SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record conforming to it is
                committed to canon/registry/** only through the Mutation Coordinator after
                G-CANON-R (Roadmap §0.8, PART X; 076 §13). Nothing here authors or commits any
                Record, and Canon: canonical-about-meaning and CD: yes license no write
                (SC-077-H). The canonicalization-source rule of Roadmap §0.8 does not apply to
                this file: rows 075, 085, 105, 106 and 107 are the canonicalization sources, and
                row 077 is a structural contract.

DOWNSTREAM      Row 077's → names all model schemas. Every concrete SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record is
                bound by this contract, whatever schema it defines. The model-owned schema
                rows — World 184, 186, 188, 190, 192, 194, 196, 199, 201, 233, 236; Epistemic
                260, 262, 264, 267, 269, 271, 273; Production 304, 306, 308, 310, 312, 315, 317,
                319, 322, 324, 326, 329, 331, 406; Visual 347, 349, 352; Issue 368, 370, 372,
                374, 376 — do not list 077 in their H. The unlock is preserved as written; no
                row's H, Val or Done is changed, and no model schema is required to have a
                SCHEMA-DEFINITION Record (SC-077-G). Row 114 lists 066–107, and so 077, in its
                H; it consumes this contract under its own Val. Row 078 (H: 076, 072) does not
                list 077 and is not changed. A downstream artifact uses this contract only as its
                own H and Val allow.

